Design of `_clause_asserts_merge(t, match_start, match_end)` returning:
- "ASSERT" → flag
- "MENTION" → skip
- "UNCLEAR" → fall back to existing logic.

Algorithm:
1. Segment t into clauses: first split into sentences at `.?!;` + whitespace (but protect abbreviations? not needed here), then within a sentence split at coordinating/contrastive boundaries (", and", ", but", ";", ":"). Actually colons often introduce the assertion ("The assertion is simple: C0 and C2 are one class") — the clause after the colon carries the merge predicate; good, we evaluate the clause containing the merge predicate, so segmentation at colon helps.
2. Find the clause containing the composite match (and possibly the merge predicate).
3. Determine the *governing* structure: is the composite inside quotes? Compute quote spans in the sentence: find pairs of `"`, `'`, `“”`, `‘’`. If inside a quote, the *matrix* clause (the clause with the outer predicate) governs: e.g. "The sentence 'X' is hereby adopted as the rev12 rule." → matrix predicate "is hereby adopted as the rule" → ASSERT. "A detector finding of the form 'X' was recorded as a false positive" → matrix "was recorded as a false positive" → MENTION. "'X' is exactly the pattern the detector must flag" → MENTION. "The F0 text says "X"; we reject that reading" → matrix "says" → MENTION (and second clause rejects).
4. Predicate lexicons (regex) applied to a clause:
   - MERGE_PRED: `\b(?:are|is|be|was|were|remains?|constitute[sd]?|form|forms|share[sd]?|treat(?:s|ed)?\s+as|unif(?:y|ies|ied)|merge[sd]?|combine[sd]?|into\s+a\s+single|one\s+class|single\s+class|one\s+schema|same\s+class|(?:no|zero)\s+distinction|not\s+distinct|not\s+separate|dropped|adopt(?:s|ed)?|hold[s]?|keep|kept|concluded)\b` — careful, this is broad. Better to define *conditioned* patterns:
     - IDENTITY: `(?:are|is|be|was|were|remains?|constitutes?|forms?|share[sd]?|treat(?:s|ed)?\s+as|unif(?:y|ies|ied)|merge[sd]?|combined?|kept as|keep as|into a single class|one class|single class|one schema|same class|one label|one regularity|one unit|our unit|the unit|adopted design|chosen unit|right unit|canonical design|no distinction|not distinct|not separate|combined)`
   - NEG_MERGE (in the same clause, scoping the merge): `(?:not|never|no|none|reject(?:s|ed)?|den(?:y|ies|ied)|refus(?:e|es|ed)|fals(?:e|ely)?|wrong|refut(?:e|es|ed)|isn't|aren't|wasn't|weren't|doesn't|don't)\b` near the merge predicate, specifically:
     - `(?:are|is|was|were|remains?)\s+(?:not|never)\b`
     - `\bno\s+(?:genuine\s+)?(?:C0/?C2|c2/?c0)\s+merge`
     - `(?:merge|claim|reading|hypothesis|finding|proposal|assertion|pattern|outcome|story)\b[^.]{0,40}\b(?:rejected|refuted|denied|false|wrong|not\s+our)`
     - `not\s+(?:a\s+)?(?:distinction|merge|class|our\s+claim)\b`
     - `non-?merge` (compound) — appears in MENTION.
   - NEG_SEPARATION (negated separation = merge assertion): `(?:not|never|rather than|as opposed to|instead of|no longer)\s+(?:\w+\s+){0,3}(?:separat\w*|split|distinct\w*|two classes|apart|hygiene)` and `(?:do|does|did)\s+not\s+separate`, `not\s+a\s+distinction\s+we\s+keep`, `kept together`, `not\s+separate`, `not\s+distinct`.
   - MENTION_FRAME (matrix or clause-level): `\b(?:says?|said|quote[sd]?|report(?:s|ed)?|record(?:s|ed)?|describe[sd]?|call(?:s|ed)?|label(?:s|ed)?|name[sd]?|phrase|token|string|sentence|pattern|hypothesis|question|whether|claim\s+that|finding|flags?|flagged|detector|false[- ]positive|non-?merge|reject(?:s|ed)?|answered\s+in\s+the\s+negative|must\s+flag|not\s+our\s+claim|never\s+write|do\s+not\s+write)\b`
   - ENDORSE_FRAME: `\b(?:we\s+agree|we\s+hold|hold\s+that|concluded\s+that|our\s+(?:position|reading|view|design|unit)|adopt(?:s|ed)?|hereby\s+adopt|is\s+our|matches\s+our|our\s+canonical|our\s+adopted|the\s+decision\s+that|it\s+has\s+been\s+asserted|and\s+we\s+agree|no\s+question|no\s+doubt|never\s+wrong|correct\s+to\s+say|froze\s+it|really\s+does|this\s+text\s+does|settled)\b`

5. Decision procedure per composite occurrence:
   a. Find the sentence containing it; split into clauses with offsets.
   b. Identify clause C containing the composite match.
   c. If C contains MERGE_IDENTITY predicate (or the sentence's *adopted clause* does):
      - If C is interrogative ("?" at clause end or starts with is/are/does/do/whether) → MENTION unless ENDORSE.
      - If C is a `whether`/`that` complement of a MENTION_FRAME verb (hypothesis/claim/question/says/rejects...) governing it → MENTION.
      - If C contains NEG_MERGE → MENTION.
      - If ENDORSE_FRAME in C or matrix → ASSERT.
      - Else ASSERT.
   d. Else if C contains NEG_SEPARATION (and not prohibition-of-writing frame) → ASSERT.
   e. Else if C contains mention-of-composite frames → MENTION.
   f. Else UNCLEAR (fallback).

